A controller may be written by inheriting from `dls::Controller`. It
communicates with the rest of the framework by reading a `dls::GaitSignal` using
`dls::Controller::readGaitSignal` and the getting the robot state using
`dls::Controller::readBlindStateSignal`. It publishes a `dls::ControlSignal`
using the call to `dls::publishSignal`.

The controller is made available to the rest of the framework by exporting it
using the macro `DLS_EXPORT_CONTROLLER`.

As a simple example, consider the following class:

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_CONTROLLER_HPP@
```

Its implementation is shown here:

```cpp
@EXAMPLE_CONTROLLER_CPP@
```

The framework will first look for the controller in the directory where the
framework was launched. This means that, during development and debugging, a
controller can be recompiled _while the framework is running_ and reloaded.

The framework will also look in predetermined installation directories for the
controller. To install it to the correct location automatically, even if these
directories were to change in future, use the following CMake code:

```txt
@EXAMPLE_CONTROLLER_CMAKE@
```
